From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ad5xrecon <ad5xrecon@localhost>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] settings: IFS firmware switch in Settings > Devices
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Settings > Devices gets an "IFS firmware" row on printers whose Klipper
publishes the `ifs_firmware` object. Its value is the firmware the IFS
runs now (Stock, Klipper or Unknown, from printer.ifs_firmware.firmware).

The row opens an overlay with the switch to the other firmware (both
when the IFS answers neither, e.g. after a failed switch), greyed with
the reason during a print (print_stats printing or paused). A confirm
dialog says what the switch does; then the screen asks Moonraker for it
(POST /server/kiln/ifs_firmware {"target": ...}, JSON-RPC
server.kiln.post_ifs_firmware). No G-code starts a switch. A refusal
(not while printing, a switch already running, bad target) is shown as
the result.

The root agent stops Klipper while it flashes, so the progress view
polls Moonraker's status of the switch (server.kiln.get_ifs_firmware)
once a second while it runs or Klipper is not back: the phase
("Writing…", the replug hint "Unplug the IFS cable and plug it back
in") and "Don't switch the printer off.". The recovery dialog ("Printer
shutdown", "Klipper disconnected") is held off from the request until
Klipper is back: the agent stops Klipper before the first poll. Once Klipper is ready the
result shows the version the IFS answers, or the agent's message. After
180 s without Klipper (or without the agent picking the request up) it
stops waiting and says where to look. The poll timer stops whenever the
overlay closes or the screen sleeps; a switch is followed again on
reopen. Klipper comes back with or without `mcu ifs` and the plugin's
objects: for 10 minutes after a request the hardware check doesn't
notify that change ("present in previous session"); it still saves the
new set for the next session.

The texts come from a pure function (ad5x_ifs_firmware_view.h) with
unit tests ([ad5x_ifs_firmware]). English strings only; other languages
fall back to English.

Upstream: not applicable (AD5X OS specific)
